nt pitch names, replacing the letters entirely. For them, “do” *is* the name of that white key just like “C” is for you. - **Germanic/English cultures** kept the letters for permanent pitch names and re‑purposed the old syllables as a movable, key‑agnostic teaching system. When you hear someone say “do-re-mi” in an English-speaking choir rehearsal, they’re usually working on hearing the major scale in whatever key the piece is in (movable). But when you see “Do # menor” on a Spanish lead sheet, they’re just writing “C# minor” in their own language (fixed). **In short: Your hunch was right. They *can* just be alternative labels for the same notes (fixed-do). But because the same syllables are also used as a relative scale-degree system (movable-do), it’s not a pure 1-to-1 equation worldwide. That’s the only reason it seems inconsistent.**
